##DESCRIPTION
## energy in simple harmonic motion
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillations)
## DBsection(Energy and Simple Harmonic Motion)
## Date(29 December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Santo D'Agostino)
## TitleText('')
## AuthorText('')
## EditionText('2018')
## Section('16.5')
## Problem('15')
## KEYWORDS(kinematics, energy, kinetic energy, elastic potential energy, mechanical energy, stiffness constant, simple harmonic motion)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->variables->set(t => 'Real');

$X  = random(15,  25,  1);
$f  = random(0.4, 2.0, 0.1);
$m  = random(0.5, 1.5, 0.1);
$k  = $m * (2 * pi * $f)**2;
$g  = Compute("$X*cos(2*pi*$f*t)");
$dg = Compute("-2*pi*$f*$X*sin(2*pi*$f*t)");

@t = (1 .. 3);
@x = map { $g->eval(t => $_) } @t;
@v = map { $dg->eval(t => $_) } @t;
@K = map { Compute("0.5 $m ($_/100)^2 ")->reduce } @v;
@U = map { Compute("0.5 $k ($_/100)^2 ")->reduce } @x;
@E = map { $K[$_] + $U[$_] } (0 .. 2);

$t1 = 1.0;
$t2 = 2.0;
$t3 = 3.0;
$x1 = $X * cos(2 * pi * $f * $t1);
$x2 = $X * cos(2 * pi * $f * $t2);
$x3 = $X * cos(2 * pi * $f * $t3);
$v1 = (-2 * pi * $f * $X) * sin(2 * pi * $f * $t1);
$v2 = (-2 * pi * $f * $X) * sin(2 * pi * $f * $t2);
$v3 = (-2 * pi * $f * $X) * sin(2 * pi * $f * $t3);
$K1 = 0.5 * $m * ($v1 / 100)**2;
$K2 = 0.5 * $m * ($v2 / 100)**2;
$K3 = 0.5 * $m * ($v3 / 100)**2;
$U1 = 0.5 * $k * ($x1 / 100)**2;
$U2 = 0.5 * $k * ($x2 / 100)**2;
$U3 = 0.5 * $k * ($x3 / 100)**2;
$E1 = $K1 + $U1;
$E2 = $K2 + $U2;
$E3 = $K3 + $U3;

BEGIN_PGML
A block attached to a spring undergoes simple harmonic motion, sliding back and forth along a straight line on a horizontal, frictionless surface. The amplitude of the block's motion is [`[$X]`] cm, the frequency of the block's motion is [`[$f]`] Hz, and the mass of the block is [`[$m]`] kg.
  
a) Determine the spring's stiffness constant.
  
    [_]{$k} N/m

b) The block is initially stretched and then released at time [`t = 0`]. Determine a formula for the position function of the block.
  
    [`x`] = [_]{$g} cm

c) Determine a formula for the velocity function of the block, where the velocity is measured in cm/s.

    [`v`] = [_]{$dg} [`\textrm{cm}/\textrm{s}`]

d) Determine the following after [`[$t[0]]\textrm{s}`]:

    i) position: [_]{$x[0]} [`\textrm{cm}`]. 

    ii) velocity: [_]{$v[0]} [`\textrm{cm}/\textrm{s}`]

    iii) kinetic energy: [_]{$K[0]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    iv) elastic potential energy: [_]{$U[0]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    v) total energy: [_]{$E[0]} [`\textrm{J}`]

e) Determine the following after [`[$t[1]]\textrm{s}`]:

    i) position: [_]{$x[1]} [`\textrm{cm}`]. 

    ii) velocity: [_]{$v[1]} [`\textrm{cm}/\textrm{s}`]

    iii) kinetic energy: [_]{$K[1]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    iv) elastic potential energy: [_]{$U[1]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    v) total energy: [_]{$E[1]} [`\textrm{J}`]

f) Determine the following after [`[$t[2]]\textrm{s}`]:

    i) position: [_]{$x[2]} [`\textrm{cm}`]. 

    ii) velocity: [_]{$v[2]} [`\textrm{cm}/\textrm{s}`]

    iii) kinetic energy: [_]{$K[2]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    iv) elastic potential energy: [_]{$U[2]} [`\textrm{J}`]

    v) total energy: [_]{$E[2]} [`\textrm{J}`]

g) Compare the results for the total mechanical energy after [`1.0`] s, [`2.0`] s, and [`3.0`] s. Is this consistent with what you know about simple harmonic motion with no dissipative forces acting? Explain.

END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();
